00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:39.209
Every five years — typically in October
or November — about 2,300 people from across

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:43.690
China travel to the Great Hall of the People
in Beijing for what’s called the “National

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:46.290
Party Congress”.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:53.769
This year, for example, is the 20th Party
Congress, which began on October 16th, 2022.

00:02:53.769 --> 00:02:59.490
Because it’s designed to appear representative
of society, most of these 2,300 people — called

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:04.000
“delegates” — are relatively low-ranking
party members.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.800
They include everyone from farmers to celebrities,
factory workers, and mayors.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:15.769
Likewise, to create the veneer of harmony,
ethnic minorities are awkwardly paraded around

00:03:15.769 --> 00:03:18.599
in their traditional dress.

00:03:18.599 --> 00:03:21.490
The whole thing is a giant spectacle.

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:26.380
There are giant red curtains with golden tassels
and more clapping than an American State of

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:28.189
the Union.

00:03:28.189 --> 00:03:33.150
The party takes no risks in the run-up to
the country’s most important political event.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:39.610
VPNs, which, although illegal, are ordinarily
tolerated for use by foreigners and the technologically

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.390
savvy — suddenly stop working.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:46.590
Dissenters are preemptively put under house
arrest.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:48.300
Police patrols are doubled.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:53.530
And private businesses — both Chinese and
foreign — are warned not to rain on the

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:56.569
party’s parade by making any major announcements.

00:03:56.569 --> 00:04:02.480
Yet, for all this showmanship, the actual
event is exceptionally boring.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:08.689
It may be theater, but not the kind anyone
would voluntarily watch.

00:04:08.689 --> 00:04:13.470
On the first day, for instance, the Party
Secretary delivers a looong speech called

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:18.479
the “Political Report”, reflecting on
the past five years and setting the priorities

00:04:18.479 --> 00:04:19.890
for the next.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:25.190
It’s here that you’re reminded how incredibly
Leninist the Communist Party still very much

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:27.210
remains.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:32.110
Rather than specific policies or plans, you
might hear about the “Three Represents”,

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:35.900
“Four Comprehensives”, or “strengthening
of confidences”.

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:41.810
It’s only by deciphering these vague slogans
that China-watchers infer where the country

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:42.810
is headed.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:47.000
It’s an entire language of its own.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:52.630
After this, the 2,300 delegates, quote, “study”
the speech, and inevitably accept it without

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:53.740
challenge.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:58.880
That’s because the real decisions have been
made much earlier.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:02.870
Although the party doesn’t acknowledge this
fact, officials meet in secret at a summer

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:06.919
resort east of Beijing in August.

00:05:06.919 --> 00:05:11.300
The second item on the agenda is to amend
the party’s constitution.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:17.270
Again, no laws are being written here, only
ideological statements.

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:22.520
In 2017, for example, Xi Jinping’s name
was added — implying that he’s not just

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:26.710
another politician, but a great figure in
party history.

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:32.850
Finally, at the end of this week comes by
far the most important task, when delegates

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.090
“elect” members of the Central Committee.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:40.680
“Elect” in quotes because, remember, almost
everything has already been decided.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:48.610
In 2017, delegates had 222 candidates to choose
from for 204 seats, giving them the option

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:50.750
to reject just 9%.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:56.900
Those 204 people then became members of the
Central Committee, the next level up in the

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:59.060
party hierarchy.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:03.400
There are almost as many “alternates'',
who attend meetings but aren’t allowed to

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:04.400
vote.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:08.580
They can replace full members when they die
or lose their positions.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:13.000
The Central Committee meets at least once
a year in what’s called a “plenum” — and

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:17.130
the first plenum occurs the day after the
Party Congress ends.

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:20.380
This year, on October 23rd.

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:25.960
Their most important task is to elect members
of the 25-member Political Bureau — or Politburo

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:30.180
— another level up in the party hierarchy.

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:36.270
The Politburo, in turn, “elects” a Standing
Committee of usually 7 or 9 people — one

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:38.940
of which is the General Secretary of the Party.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:42.400
In this case, Xi Jinping.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:47.470
Notice the phrase “General Secretary”,
not “President”.

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:53.340
China is a Party-State, which means it has
a government and a single ruling party.

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:58.060
The two mirror each other in many ways, but
they aren’t the same.

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:02.270
The state, confusingly, has its own version
of the National Party Congress called the

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:04.599
National People’s Congress.

00:07:04.599 --> 00:07:10.001
And to make things even more confusing, multiple
parties participate in the People’s Congress

00:07:10.001 --> 00:07:13.099
— not just the Communist Party.

00:07:13.099 --> 00:07:18.270
But they’re all members of a single “United
Front”, which means they co-exist, not compete

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.069
with the CCP.

00:07:20.069 --> 00:07:26.160
Anyway, just as the Party congress helps elect
a General Secretary every 5 years in the Fall

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:30.900
on the Party side, the People’s congress
elects the country’s President and Premier

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:34.330
every 5 years in the Spring on the State side.

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:40.100
Though, don’t think of the party and state
as entirely separate, either — there’s

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:42.160
lots of overlap.

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:46.660
Virtually everyone working for the Chinese
state is a Party member, but not all Party

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:49.000
members work for the state.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:53.370
Afterall, there are nearly 100 million party
members.

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:58.000
As General Secretary, Xi Jinping is head of
the Party.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:00.860
And as President, he’s head of state.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:05.270
These are two different roles that could be
filled by two different people.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:10.970
But they’re not, which gives him the choice
of which to use at any given moment.

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:16.460
When Xi visits a foreign country — unless
that country is, say, Communist Cuba, or North

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:20.099
Korea — he does so as the “President”
of China.

00:08:20.099 --> 00:08:24.830
That’s technically true — he is, in fact,
the President.

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:30.270
But there’s a growing movement in favor
of always calling him “General Secretary”.

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:33.470
In China, the Party is above the state.

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:38.280
The People’s Liberation Army reports directly
to the Party, not the government.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:45.180
A city’s Party Secretary is given the license
plate number “1”, and its mayor, “2”.

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:48.800
Trivial as this may sound, it is telling.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:53.390
The CCP even has its own, separate justice
system.

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:58.480
In other words, Xi Jinping’s most important
titles are General Secretary and head of the

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:03.529
military, not President, which is mostly a
ceremonial role.

00:09:03.529 --> 00:09:08.601
The common use of “President”, many argue,
is a deliberate strategy on China’s part

00:09:08.601 --> 00:09:10.910
to obscure the Party’s importance.

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:15.970
It wants to appear to investors like any other
country, not one where your private property

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:18.930
might be confiscated for political reasons.

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:23.830
But, in truth, even the title “General Secretary”
is a bit misleading.

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:30.460
In most countries, a person’s position in
government is what makes them powerful.

00:09:30.460 --> 00:09:35.270
Joe Biden is powerful because and — (for
the most part) — only because he’s the

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:37.130
President of the United States.

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:43.490
Sure, former Presidents are celebrities, but
fame is not to be confused with power.

00:09:43.490 --> 00:09:46.700
This all probably sounds exceptionally obvious.

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:49.709
But it’s not true in China.

00:09:49.709 --> 00:09:54.670
Take an example: Have you ever heard or seen
a photo of Deng Xiaoping?

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:58.310
What about Zhao Ziyang?

00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:03.200
Chances are the answer is ‘yes’ to the
first question and ‘no’ to the second.

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:09.300
Deng is the iconic pioneer of China’s reform
era, while Zhao is forgotten by all but students

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:10.460
of history.

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:16.050
Yet Deng Xiaoping was never General Secretary,
Zhao was.

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:22.210
Instead, Deng is referred to as a “Paramount
Leader”, an informal nickname, not an official

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:23.800
position.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:29.140
When Deng famously traveled to Shenzhen calling
for more economic reform, he was 87 years

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:33.940
old and fully retired yet powerful enough
to steer the country’s direction.

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:39.950
You don’t need any specific title to wield
considerable political power in China — even

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:46.080
the most political power — and, conversely,
neither does holding any specific title guarantee

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:47.339
power.

00:10:47.339 --> 00:10:52.310
The “watershed” moment in Chinese history,
when Jiang Zemin peacefully handed over power

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:56.750
to Hu Jintao in 2002, is not quite what it
seems.

00:10:56.750 --> 00:11:02.690
In reality, Jiang quietly stayed on as head
of the military until he was heavily pressured

00:11:02.690 --> 00:11:06.110
to step down in 2004.

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:10.430
Like China’s “embrace” of Capitalism,
the party’s conformity to consistent rules

00:11:10.430 --> 00:11:17.750
and norms has always been a mix of both our
wishful thinking and their skillful performance.

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:22.070
Even age limits — which were seen as a positive
sign that no one would be allowed to rule

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:26.329
forever, turned out to merely be a way for
Jiang Zemin to purge his rivals.

00:11:26.329 --> 00:11:30.570
And the limit has been broken when convenient
since.

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:37.209
When China made headlines in 2018 for removing
term limits, that was for the office of President.

00:11:37.209 --> 00:11:42.120
There have never been term limits on Party
Secretaries, meaning Xi Jinping could’ve

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:44.860
already stayed in power indefinitely.

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:51.180
What all this means is that in China, power
isn’t automatically conferred by office

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:54.330
— it has to be won.

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:59.450
Its leaders generally arrive relatively weak
and slowly try to consolidate authority over

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:00.960
time.

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:07.610
It took Jiang Zemin nearly six years to wrestle
control after being named General Secretary.

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:11.220
Hu Jintao arguably never did.

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:16.760
What makes Xi Jinping unique is not so much
his hunger for power but the speed with which

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:19.110
he acquired it.

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:23.940
As we’ve seen, there were very few actual
rules preventing him from accumulating power.

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:30.910
It was simply a question of political skill
and luck — both of which he had in droves.

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:35.440
Xi is what you might call a “princeling”
— his father was a revolutionary fighter

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:38.139
and high-ranking Party official.

00:12:38.139 --> 00:12:44.200
Meaning, as a child, he had both privilege
and, likely, a sense of entitlement — his

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:47.529
“red-blood” made him “destined” for
the throne.

00:12:47.529 --> 00:12:53.680
Now, make no mistake — princelings have
shifted in and out of favor over time.

00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:58.389
As a teenager during the Cultural Revolution,
Xi was sent to “learn from the proletariat”

00:12:58.389 --> 00:13:01.709
by doing manual labor in the countryside.

00:13:01.709 --> 00:13:05.269
He lived in a cave, and his father was arrested.

00:13:05.269 --> 00:13:11.589
Later, after Mao’s death in 1976, princelings
were “revived” as the party revisited

00:13:11.589 --> 00:13:12.920
its past.

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:19.050
Xi, like many of his elite peers, landed a
promising job in government.

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:23.510
But unlike his peers, Xi Jinping kept his
head down.

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:28.410
Thanks to their connections, princelings had
no trouble securing jobs, but very rarely

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:32.620
were they promoted to the very top, where
their superiors thought they might cause trouble

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:35.260
or even threaten to replace them.

00:13:35.260 --> 00:13:41.490
Instead, says expert in Chinese politics Victor
Shih, they tended to promote, quote, “always

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:45.970
nice people” — weak cadres who obediently
followed orders.

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:51.780
Xi, on the other hand, avoided the spotlight
by taking relatively low-profile jobs outside

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:55.890
of the city — where he quietly climbed the
ranks.

00:13:55.890 --> 00:14:00.899
By the time he reached the apex of power in
2012, not only was he better connected than

00:14:00.899 --> 00:14:05.709
most of his peers, but, ironically, he also
benefited from this discrimination against

00:14:05.709 --> 00:14:10.610
princelings — he didn’t have much competition!

00:14:10.610 --> 00:14:15.620
These are all the Politburo members before
he became General Secretary — ranked according

00:14:15.620 --> 00:14:20.810
to the size of their political networks — basically:
his competitors.

00:14:20.810 --> 00:14:27.949
First, we can remove anyone due for retirement
— they, obviously, aren’t much of a threat.

00:14:27.949 --> 00:14:35.010
Next, let’s remove all the non-princelings,
who lack the connections and privileges.

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:40.269
Institutional sexism effectively disqualifies
all women.

00:14:40.269 --> 00:14:45.110
We can also remove this person because of
a reputational stain — his brother defected

00:14:45.110 --> 00:14:47.759
to the United States.

00:14:47.759 --> 00:14:51.640
That leaves Xi with only 3 major competitors.

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:56.940
Bo Xilai was subsequently arrested, and the
other two were quietly demoted to the unimportant

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:58.750
Vice President position.

00:14:58.750 --> 00:15:04.240
He then reorganized the military, launched
an enormous anti-corruption campaign that

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:09.029
helped purge rivals, and enshrined his name
in the party constitution.

00:15:09.029 --> 00:15:15.360
Today, Xi Jinping is effectively unchallenged,
making him more powerful than any Chinese

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:17.000
leader since Mao.

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:20.449
Yet, Xi is not Mao.

00:15:20.449 --> 00:15:24.850
The challenge to understanding China is learning
from its past without losing sight of the

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:30.730
present — which is what I try to do in my
Nebula Original series “China, Actually”.

00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:36.050
Each video we try to solve a puzzle about
China — like why some protests are tolerated

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:42.680
and others not, or why it’s still trying
to maintain zero cases of COVID in late 2022.

